{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>For your servant became surety for the lad.</b> I entered as surety on his behalf and accepted upon myself a condition that he has no redemption all the days — each of my brothers is outside and I am inside, to be banished in two worlds. And this is why he mentioned 'all the days' — in this world and in the world to come, in the language of the verse (Deuteronomy 6:24): 'for our good all the days,' whose meaning is thus." "<b>And it appears</b> that since he mentioned the expression 'to you,' it would have been fitting to say 'and I shall have sinned against you'; and furthermore, for thus he said to him (Genesis 43): 'if I do not bring him to you and set him before you, and I shall have sinned against you all the days' — but he said 'and I shall have sinned against my father,' as if to say 'and I shall have sinned against Elohim,' including Jacob his father and his Father in heaven. For since he had already mentioned Elohim to them — 'I fear Elohim' — Judah informs us that he is bound by this in a strong and mighty bond, to be plundering his fathers and sinning against both of them in two worlds. And this is what he said, 'all the days,' that is, in all the days." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'For your servant became surety for the lad' — this is what the verse says (Proverbs 6:1): 'My son, if you have struck hands for your friend…' The way of the surety is to ask of the borrower, 'Do you wish that I enter as surety for you?' — upon this it says, 'you are snared by the words of your mouth.' And the way of the surety is also that he acknowledges the lender in this matter, and upon this it says (ibid.), 'you are caught by the words of your mouth.' 'Do this now, my son, and deliver yourself, when you have come into the hand of your friend: go, humble yourself, and urge your friend.' 'Your friend' — the second party is the lender; that is, he must humble himself before both of them — before the lender, that he wait for him, and before the borrower, that he release him from the surety. And about whom was this verse said? About Judah, who was surety for Jacob his father, as he said, 'For your servant became surety for the lad.' Joseph said to him: Just as you became surety for Benjamin, why did you not become surety for your brother? For I see in my goblet that you sold your brother for money and grieved your father, until he said, 'A wild beast has torn him.' When Judah heard this, he cried out in a loud voice and said, 'For how shall I go up to my father…' He said to him: Come, let us reason together, the two of us. Say your words and arrange your weapons. Judah said to Naphtali: Go and see how many markets there are in Egypt. He leapt and returned and said: Twelve. Judah said to his brothers: I will destroy three of them; each of you take one market. They said to him: Judah, Egypt is not like Shechem — if you destroy Egypt, you will destroy the entire world. He said to him: At first you came against us with a false charge and said, 'You are spies'; second, 'you came to see the nakedness of the land'; third, 'you stole the goblet.' I swear by the life of my old father, and you swear by the life of Pharaoh the wicked — if I draw my sword from its sheath, I will fill all of Egypt with corpses! Joseph said to him: If you draw your sword, I will bind it around your neck. Judah said to him: If I open my mouth, I will swallow you. Joseph said to him: If you open your mouth, I will stop it with a stone. Judah said: What shall we say to our father? Joseph said to him: Say to him — the rope went after the bucket. He said to him: You are judging us with a false judgment! He said to him: There is no false judgment — only the sale of your brother. Judah said: The fiery anger of Shechem burns in my heart. Joseph said to him: The fiery anger of Tamar your daughter-in-law — I am dripping it. Judah said: My anger is boiling and there is none who can restrain me. Joseph said: Your boiling anger — I am extinguishing it. Judah said: Now I will dye the markets of Egypt with blood! He said to him: You dye all your days — thus you dyed your brother's tunic in blood and said to your father, 'Joseph has surely been torn.' They all agreed to destroy Egypt. When Joseph saw this, he said: I must reveal myself to them so they do not destroy Egypt. Immediately, 'And Joseph said to his brothers, I am Joseph your brother.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:26.194206440Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}